 "baseline_margin": -6.11, "analyst_adjustment": -0.89, "key_factors": ["R+6 district lean with incumbent Flood's fundraising edge ($2.4M vs $0.6M)", "Expert consensus clusters at Likely/Solid R, though two raters see it closer", "A September Democratic-sponsored poll showed a one-point race, hinting at a competitive environment", "Prediction markets price Flood near 80% to win"], "rationale": "The district's R+6 lean, Flood's incumbency and dominant fundraising, and a Likely R expert consensus point to a clear but not safe Republican hold. A late Democratic-sponsored poll showing a tied race and an active debate exchange justify keeping this at Lean R rather than Likely R, with meaningful uncertainty.", "watch": "Any independent or follow-up polling confirming the tight September survey would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Nebraska's 1st District favors Republicans by about six points, and first-term incumbent Mike Flood holds large advantages in money and name recognition. He has raised roughly four times as much as Democrat Chris Backemeyer and enters the fall with a wide cash lead, while no outside spending has materialized for the challenger.\n\nStill, the race has drawn attention: a September poll from a Democratic sponsor showed the candidates essentially tied, and the two met in a contentious late-September debate over Iran, tariffs, Medicaid and immigration. Expert raters mostly call the seat Likely or Solid Republican, and prediction markets price Flood around an 80% favorite. The forecast lands on a Republican win of roughly seven points, competitive enough to watch but with Flood clearly favored.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1206, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2177, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.16